From 8b85c9086bd50aa428c11423987edb5f42a0260c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: taekim <taekim@day34.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:00:09 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] Export a drawing's paths, whether it sits in the text,
 and what a fill really is
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A drawing arrived as {"label":"shape","w":0,"h":0} — no geometry, not even a
size — so 78 of them in one manual were 78 blanks. rhwp's own renderer already
flattens every shape kind to move/line/curve/close before it draws, so that same
reduction is what ships, with stroke, fill and arrowheads.

treat_as_char rides along because a host with no floating layer must not inline
an anchored object: several of those shapes are the size of the whole page, and
inlining them all costs 29 pages.

And a fill is a PICTURE far more often than a colour — 352 of that file's 821
definitions, against 53 solid ones. Exporting only the colour rendered every
rounded frame in the document as blank paper.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
